.TITLE "journalctl"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Queries and displays logs from the systemd journal. It is the centralized tool for __inspecting system logs__, service errors, and kernel messages in modern Linux.

.DIV "TLDR"
**journalctl**
Displays the entire system journal, starting from the oldest entry.
> 

**journalctl -u service**
Displays logs only for a specific systemd service.
> __-u__ unit flag | __service__ the name of the service

**journalctl -f**
Displays new log entries in real-time as they are added to the journal.
> __-f__ follow flag

**journalctl -b**
Displays only the logs from the current system boot.
> __-b__ boot flag

**journalctl -p err**
Filters the logs to show only entries with a priority of error or higher.
> __-p__ priority flag | __err__ error level

.DIV "SUMMARY"
The journal is stored in a binary format, so you must use __journalctl__ to read it. Combining __-u__ with __-f__ is the most effective way to debug a service that is currently failing or behaving unexpectedly.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**cli:systemctl** **cli:dmesg** **cli:tail**

